const imp=new Set([1,2,3,4,5,8,11,12,17,19,21,23,27,28,29,30]);
const ex={
1:"Asks if the candidate understands the 3 levels of GenAI apps. A <b>single LLM call</b> is like calling one REST API: you send a prompt and get text back, and the model only knows what it learned in training. <b>RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)</b> first searches your own documents (usually a vector DB), then pastes the relevant pieces into the prompt so the answer is based on your data. A <b>multi-agent system</b> is several LLM 'workers', each with its own role and tools (APIs, DB, code), handing work to each other like microservices. The second part checks judgment: when is the extra complexity worth it?",
2:"<b>LangChain</b> is a Python/JS library for building LLM apps; a <b>chain</b> is a fixed pipeline of steps (prompt, then LLM, then parser), like a straight line. <b>LangGraph</b> (from the same team) models the app as a <b>graph</b>: nodes are steps, edges decide what runs next, and a shared <b>state</b> object is passed around. Think of it like a Spring State Machine or a workflow engine. Agents need loops (try, check result, try again), which a straight chain can't do cleanly.",
3:"<b>MCP (Model Context Protocol)</b> is a new standard, released by Anthropic in late 2024, for plugging tools and data into any AI app. Before MCP, every app wrote its own 'function calling' glue: you describe a function in JSON, the LLM asks to call it, and your code calls it. With MCP you build an <b>MCP server</b> once (like a small REST service that wraps your internal API) and any MCP-compatible client (Claude, Cursor, custom agents) can discover and use it. The question checks whether they know the difference: reusable standard vs. one-off, per-app code.",
4:"The job description mentions a product that checks requirements (user stories/specs) for problems and fixes them automatically. This is a design question: how would they split the work across agents? For example, one agent reads the requirement, one checks it for gaps and ambiguity, one rewrites it, and one reviews the result. It also asks how the agents talk to each other: a <b>shared memory</b> (a common state everyone reads and writes), <b>message passing</b> (agents send messages to each other), or a <b>supervisor pattern</b> (a boss agent assigns tasks and collects results).",
5:"Agents decide on their own what to do next, so they can get stuck repeating the same step forever, and every step costs money (tokens). The question asks what hard safety limits they put in place. It's like protecting a service against an infinite loop or a runaway batch job.",
6:"<b>Short-term memory</b> is what the agent remembers within the current chat (like an HTTP session). <b>Long-term memory</b> is what it remembers across days or sessions (like a database). A <b>vector DB</b> stores text as numeric 'embeddings' so you can search by meaning, not exact words. The question checks whether they know where each type of memory lives and how to fetch it back.",
7:"'Self-healing' means that when the agent's output fails (for example, a generated test automation script crashes because a button locator changed), the agent notices, figures out why, and fixes itself without a human. Check that they have a clear way to <b>detect</b> failure (errors, test results) and a loop to <b>repair and retry</b> with a limit.",
8:"Lots of people build demos (PoCs). This question checks whether they know how to <b>prove</b> an AI system is good enough for real users. Think of it as unit and regression testing for AI: a fixed test set with expected answers, metrics, and automated checks in the CI pipeline.",
9:"<b>Vector search</b> finds text with similar meaning ('car' matches 'vehicle') but is bad at exact matches like account numbers or error codes. <b>Keyword search (BM25)</b> is classic search-engine exact matching. <b>SQL</b> is for structured data such as amounts and dates. <b>Hybrid RAG</b> combines them to get the best of each. The question checks whether they know when each one fails.",
10:"Every LLM call costs money (priced per token, roughly per word) and takes time (often seconds). An agent may make 5–20 calls per request. The question asks how they make it cheaper and faster, the same idea as optimizing slow microservice calls.",
11:"This is a bank, so the agent must follow strict rules. <b>RBAC</b> (role-based access control) means the agent can only do what the user is allowed to do. <b>Audit logging</b> means recording everything for regulators. <b>PII redaction</b> means hiding personal data (names, account numbers, PAN/Aadhaar) before it is sent to the LLM. A strong answer also covers <b>prompt injection</b>, where a user tricks the AI into ignoring its rules.",
12:"This is an end-to-end design question for a practical use case: given a Jira user story, automatically produce test cases. Ask them to walk through each stage: how the story comes in, what the agent does, how the output is checked for quality, and where the result ends up. Similar to designing any data pipeline or microservice flow.",
13:"This is the DevOps side. How do they package the agent (Docker), run it at scale (Kubernetes), and deploy it automatically (CI/CD such as Jenkins or GitHub Actions)? The key AI-specific part is <b>observability</b>: being able to see each step the agent took and each tool it called, like distributed tracing (Zipkin/Jaeger) in microservices.",
14:"Prompts are effectively code: changing one sentence can change the app's behavior. The question asks how they manage prompts and agent settings safely across dev, QA, and prod. It's like managing application.yml and Spring profiles, plus Git reviews and rollback.",
15:"Classic warm-up DSA question. <b>Reverse</b>: flip the next pointers so the list runs backwards. <b>Detect cycle</b>: check whether following next ever loops back (Floyd's 'tortoise and hare': one pointer moves 1 step, the other 2, and if they meet there is a loop).",
16:"This is the well-known LeetCode problem 'Longest substring without repeating characters', dressed up with tool names. Input: a list like [search, db, search, email, api]. Find the longest continuous stretch where no tool repeats. The expected technique is a <b>sliding window</b> (two pointers plus a HashMap).",
17:"<b>LRU (Least Recently Used) cache</b>: a fixed-size cache that, when full, throws out the item that hasn't been used for the longest time. It's relevant because caching LLM responses saves cost. The expected answer is get/put in O(1) time using a HashMap plus a doubly linked list (or LinkedHashMap in Java).",
18:"<b>Topological sort</b>: given tasks with dependencies ('B needs A first'), produce a valid order to run them. Like Maven resolving module build order. Works only when there are no cycles (a DAG).",
19:"Agents hand work to each other (A to B to C). If C hands back to A, it might loop forever. This asks for cycle detection in a <b>directed</b> graph, a standard DFS question, tied directly to the earlier question about agents stuck in infinite loops.",
20:"This is a theory question with no coding. A <b>HashMap</b> gives very fast exact lookups. A <b>Trie</b> (prefix tree) stores strings character by character and is great for 'find everything starting with abc' (autocomplete). The question checks whether they pick the data structure based on the type of query.",
21:"Given a table of every tool call, for EACH agent find its 3 most-used tools. The trick is 'per agent': a plain LIMIT 3 gives the top 3 overall, which is wrong. The expected answer is GROUP BY plus a window function (ROW_NUMBER/DENSE_RANK with PARTITION BY agent_id).",
22:"A 'handoff' is when a conversation moves from one agent to another. Find sessions where that happened more than N times. They need to define what counts as a handoff and then use GROUP BY + HAVING (or LAG to compare consecutive messages). Good candidates ask clarifying questions about the table columns.",
23:"<b>Window functions</b> compute values across related rows while keeping every row, unlike GROUP BY, which squashes rows together. The example asks for a running total of token cost within each session, ordered by time.",
24:"Basic SQL JOINs. The special pattern <b>LEFT JOIN ... WHERE right.id IS NULL</b> finds rows that have NO match in the other table, for example user stories that were never validated. Checks practical SQL knowledge.",
25:"A huge log table (millions of rows) is slow to search. Which index would they create for queries like 'events for agent X between two dates'? Expected answer: a composite index (agent_id, created_at), with an explanation of why the column order matters, plus possibly table partitioning by date.",
26:"Python fundamentals used heavily in AI frameworks. <b>Decorator</b>: a wrapper that adds behavior to a function (like Spring AOP annotations). <b>Generator</b>: produces values one at a time with yield (used to stream LLM output). <b>async/await</b>: non-blocking calls so many API calls can run at once. <b>GIL</b>: a Python lock that allows only one thread to run Python code at a time. It's fine for waiting on APIs, but bad for heavy CPU work.",
27:"This is your area, so dig deep here. It checks whether they really know Spring Boot: dependency injection (how Spring creates and wires beans), building REST endpoints, and how they'd put an AI agent behind a REST API as a proper microservice (config, timeouts, error handling, streaming).",
28:"This is a low-level design (LLD) question. How would they design an <b>Agent</b> class so new <b>Tools</b> (search, DB, email, and so on) can be added without changing the Agent code? Expected answer: a Tool interface plus a registry, following the Strategy pattern and the Open/Closed principle.",
29:"External APIs fail randomly. <b>Exponential backoff</b>: retry after waiting 1s, then 2s, then 4s, and so on. <b>Circuit breaker</b>: after too many failures, stop calling the service for a while and fail fast, so a broken dependency doesn't take your system down with it (Resilience4j in Spring).",
30:"This is a behavioral question. Have they actually shipped real software to users, or only built demos? Listen for concrete details: what extra work production needed (testing, monitoring, security, scaling). Use STAR: Situation, Task, Action, Result.",
31:"This is a soft skills and leadership question for a 5-year level. How would they teach a junior (or a Java developer new to AI) to write good prompts? Look for a structured teaching approach and practical tips.",
32:"This is a communication question. A manager or business person asks 'why did the AI reject this?'. Can they explain it simply, in terms of business impact, with evidence and honesty about limits?"
};
